<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Formula of Concord</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1577">1577</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Formula of Concord is a Lutheran confession of faith completed in 1577 to settle the doctrinal disputes that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s death. Drafted chiefly by Jakob Andreae and Martin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a> with the support of Elector Augustus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, it was published with earlier confessions in the Book of Concord of 1580 and became the standard of Lutheran orthodoxy in much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The work was undertaken at the behest of Elector August of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. A group of seventeen Lutheran theologians and churchmen met at Torgau, the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> government, from 9 April to 7 June 1576. With two earlier texts before them, the Swabian-Saxon Concord that Jakob Andreä had drafted in 1574 and the Maulbron Formula of 1576, they produced the Torgau Book, which Andreä condensed into what became the Epitome.</p><p>Over the following eleven months suggested changes were sent to Andreä and Martin Chemnitz. Six of the theologians, Andreä, Chemnitz, Nikolaus Selnecker, David Chytraeus, Andreas Musculus and Christoph Körner, then met at Bergen Abbey near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> in March and May 1577 and revised the text into its final form, the Bergen Book or Solid Declaration. It was presented to the elector on 28 May 1577. This is why the Formula is also called the Bergic or Bergen Book.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Formula has two parts. The Epitome sets out its twelve articles briefly; the Solid Declaration, also translated as the Thorough Declaration, explains them in detail. Approved teaching is stated in theses and rejected teaching in antitheses. The articles cover original sin, free will, the righteousness of faith before God, good works, law and gospel, the third use of the law, the Holy Supper of Christ, the person of Christ, Christ's descent into hell, church ceremonies regarded as indifferent things (adiaphora), God's eternal foreknowledge and election, and other heresies and sects. It was written in German, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation was made for the Latin edition of the Book of Concord in 1584.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Formula was signed by three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, twenty dukes and princes, twenty-four counts, four barons, thirty-five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free imperial cities</a> and more than 8,000 pastors, about two-thirds of the Lutheran Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> at the time. In the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> every clergyman had to sign or set down his objections, and a rhyme of the day ran: Write, dear Sir, write, that you might remain at the parish.</p><p>Not all Lutherans accepted it. It was rejected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a>, Anhalt, Pommerania, Holstein, Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>, Strassburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, and the government of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I of England</a> lobbied through its embassies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> against its acceptance by the German estates. Where it was received, its promulgation and subscription did much to unify and preserve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Formula of Concord” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
